{"surah": 98, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "لَمْ يَكُنِ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ مِنْ أَهْلِ ٱلْكِتَٰبِ وَٱلْمُشْرِكِينَ مُنفَكِّينَ حَتَّىٰ تَأْتِيَهُمُ ٱلْبَيِّنَةُ ", "translation": "Those who disbelieved among the People of the Scripture and the polytheists were not to be parted [from misbelief][1] until there came to them clear evidence –", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَمْ", "translit": "ْmَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lam"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَكُنِ", "translit": "ِnُkَy", "root": "كون", "latinTranslit": "yakuni"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر", "latinTranslit": "kafarū"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَهْلِ", "translit": "ِlْhَaʾ", "root": "اهل", "latinTranslit": "ahli"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب", "latinTranslit": "l-kitābi"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَٱلْمُشْرِكِينَ", "translit": "َnyِkِrْhsُmْlٱَw", "root": "شرك", "latinTranslit": "wal-mush'rikīna"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مُنفَكِّينَ", "translit": "َnyِّkَfnُm", "root": "فكك", "latinTranslit": "munfakkīna"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ḥattā"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "تَأْتِيَهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhَyِtْaʾَt", "root": "اتي", "latinTranslit": "tatiyahumu"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْبَيِّنَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَnِّyَbْlٱ", "root": "بين", "latinTranslit": "l-bayinatu"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "Those who disbelieve among the People of the Book and the idolaters were not about to change their ways until they were sent clear evidence", "clearyTranslation": "Those who disbelieve among the People of the Book and the idolaters were not about to change their ways until they were sent clear evidence"}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "رَسُولٌ مِّنَ ٱللَّهِ يَتْلُوا۟ صُحُفًا مُّطَهَّرَةً ", "translation": "A Messenger from Allāh, reciting purified[1] scriptures", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "رَسُولٌ", "translit": "ٌlwُsَr", "root": "رسل", "latinTranslit": "rasūlun"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mina"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahi"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَتْلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlْtَy", "root": "تلو", "latinTranslit": "yatlū"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "صُحُفًا", "translit": "āًfُḥُṣ", "root": "صحف", "latinTranslit": "ṣuḥufan"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مُّطَهَّرَةً", "translit": "ًẗَrَّhَṭُّm", "root": "طهر", "latinTranslit": "muṭahharatan"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "a messenger from God, reading out pages [blessed with] purity", "clearyTranslation": "a messenger from God, reading out pages [blessed with] purity"}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "فِيهَا كُتُبٌ قَيِّمَةٌ ", "translation": "Within which are correct writings [i.e., rulings and laws].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fīhā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كُتُبٌ", "translit": "ٌbُtُk", "root": "كتب", "latinTranslit": "kutubun"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَيِّمَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَmِّyَq", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "qayyimatun"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "containing true scriptures", "clearyTranslation": "containing true scriptures"}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "وَمَا تَفَرَّقَ ٱلَّذِينَ أُوتُوا۟ ٱلْكِتَٰبَ إِلَّا مِنۢ بَعْدِ مَا جَآءَتْهُمُ ٱلْبَيِّنَةُ ", "translation": "Nor did those who were given the Scripture become divided[1] until after there had come to them clear evidence.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَفَرَّقَ", "translit": "َqَّrَfَt", "root": "فرق", "latinTranslit": "tafarraqa"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أُوتُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُtwُaʾ", "root": "اتي", "latinTranslit": "ūtū"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبَ", "translit": "َbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب", "latinTranslit": "l-kitāba"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "illā"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بَعْدِ", "translit": "ِdْعَb", "root": "بعد", "latinTranslit": "baʿdi"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "mā"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "جَآءَتْهُمُ", "translit": "ُmُhْtَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا", "latinTranslit": "jāathumu"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْبَيِّنَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَnِّyَbْlٱ", "root": "بين", "latinTranslit": "l-bayinatu"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "[Yet] those who were given the Scripture became divided only after they were sent [such] clear evi-dence", "clearyTranslation": "[Yet] those who were given the Scripture became divided only after they were sent [such] clear evi-dence"}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "وَمَآ أُمِرُوٓا۟ إِلَّا لِيَعْبُدُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ مُخْلِصِينَ لَهُ ٱلدِّينَ حُنَفَآءَ وَيُقِيمُوا۟ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ وَيُؤْتُوا۟ ٱلزَّكَوٰةَۚ وَذَٰلِكَ دِينُ ٱلْقَيِّمَةِ ", "translation": "And they were not commanded except to worship Allāh, [being] sincere to Him in religion, inclining to truth, and to establish prayer and to give zakāh. And that is the correct religion.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wamā"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أُمِرُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُrِmُaʾ", "root": "امر", "latinTranslit": "umirū"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "illā"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِيَعْبُدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdُbْعَyِl", "root": "عبد", "latinTranslit": "liyaʿbudū"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-laha"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مُخْلِصِينَ", "translit": "َnyِṣِlْhkُm", "root": "خلص", "latinTranslit": "mukh'liṣīna"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَهُ", "translit": "ُhَl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "lahu"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلدِّينَ", "translit": "َnyِّdlٱ", "root": "دين", "latinTranslit": "l-dīna"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "حُنَفَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَfَnُḥ", "root": "حنف", "latinTranslit": "ḥunafāa"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَيُقِيمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmyِqُyَw", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "wayuqīmū"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَlَّṣlٱ", "root": "صلو", "latinTranslit": "l-ṣalata"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَيُؤْتُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُtْwʾُyَw", "root": "اتي", "latinTranslit": "wayu'tū"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلزَّكَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَkَّzlٱ", "root": "زكو", "latinTranslit": "l-zakata"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "وَذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhdَw", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "wadhālika"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "دِينُ", "translit": "ُnyِd", "root": "دين", "latinTranslit": "dīnu"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلْقَيِّمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmِّyَqْlٱ", "root": "قوم", "latinTranslit": "l-qayimati"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "though all they are ordered to do is worship God alone, sincerely devoting their religion to Him as people of true faith, keep up the prayer, and pay the prescribed alms, for that is the true religion", "clearyTranslation": "though all they are ordered to do is worship God alone, sincerely devoting their religion to Him as people of true faith, keep up the prayer, and pay the prescribed alms, for that is the true religion"}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ مِنْ أَهْلِ ٱلْكِتَٰبِ وَٱلْمُشْرِكِينَ فِى نَارِ جَهَنَّمَ خَٰلِدِينَ فِيهَآۚ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ هُمْ شَرُّ ٱلْبَرِيَّةِ ", "translation": "Indeed, they who disbelieved among the People of the Scripture and the polytheists will be in the fire of Hell, abiding eternally therein. Those are the worst of creatures.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَفَرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrَfَk", "root": "كفر", "latinTranslit": "kafarū"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَهْلِ", "translit": "ِlْhَaʾ", "root": "اهل", "latinTranslit": "ahli"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْكِتَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰtِkْlٱ", "root": "كتب", "latinTranslit": "l-kitābi"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱلْمُشْرِكِينَ", "translit": "َnyِkِrْhsُmْlٱَw", "root": "شرك", "latinTranslit": "wal-mush'rikīna"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fī"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "نَارِ", "translit": "ِrāَn", "root": "نور", "latinTranslit": "nāri"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّnَhَjg", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "jahannama"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "خَٰلِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِlَٰhk", "root": "خلد", "latinTranslit": "khālidīna"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فِيهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhyِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fīhā"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ulāika"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "hum"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "شَرُّ", "translit": "ُّrَhs", "root": "شرر", "latinTranslit": "sharru"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلْبَرِيَّةِ", "translit": "ِẗَّyِrَbْlٱ", "root": "برا", "latinTranslit": "l-bariyati"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "Those who disbelieve among the People of the Book and the idolaters will have the Fire of Hell, there to remain. They are the worst of creation", "clearyTranslation": "Those who disbelieve among the People of the Book and the idolaters will have the Fire of Hell, there to remain. They are the worst of creation"}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ هُمْ خَيْرُ ٱلْبَرِيَّةِ ", "translation": "Indeed, they who have believed and done righteous deeds - those are the best of creatures.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "inna"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "alladhīna"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن", "latinTranslit": "āmanū"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَعَمِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِmَعَw", "root": "عمل", "latinTranslit": "waʿamilū"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح", "latinTranslit": "l-ṣāliḥāti"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ", "translit": "َkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ulāika"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "hum"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "خَيْرُ", "translit": "ُrْyَhk", "root": "خير", "latinTranslit": "khayru"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْبَرِيَّةِ", "translit": "ِẗَّyِrَbْlٱ", "root": "برا", "latinTranslit": "l-bariyati"}], "hasIhya": false, "haleemTranslation": "Those who believe and do good deeds are the best of creation", "clearyTranslation": "Those who believe and do good deeds are the best of creation"}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "جَزَآؤُهُمْ عِندَ رَبِّهِمْ جَنَّٰتُ عَدْنٍ تَجْرِى مِن تَحْتِهَا ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ خَٰلِدِينَ فِيهَآ أَبَدًاۖ رَّضِىَ ٱللَّهُ عَنْهُمْ وَرَضُوا۟ عَنْهُۚ ذَٰلِكَ لِمَنْ خَشِىَ رَبَّهُۥ ", "translation": "Their reward with their Lord will be gardens of perpetual residence beneath which rivers flow, wherein they will abide forever, Allāh being pleased with them and they with Him. That is for whoever has feared his Lord.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "جَزَآؤُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُwʾٓāَzَjg", "root": "جزي", "latinTranslit": "jazāuhum"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِع", "root": "عند", "latinTranslit": "ʿinda"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَبِّهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب", "latinTranslit": "rabbihim"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "جَنَّٰتُ", "translit": "ُtَّٰnَjg", "root": "جنن", "latinTranslit": "jannātu"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَدْنٍ", "translit": "ٍnْdَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿadnin"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَجْرِى", "translit": "àِrْjgَt", "root": "جري", "latinTranslit": "tajrī"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "min"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَحْتِهَا", "translit": "āَhِtْḥَt", "root": "تحت", "latinTranslit": "taḥtihā"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ", "translit": "ُrَٰhْnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نهر", "latinTranslit": "l-anhāru"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "خَٰلِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِlَٰhk", "root": "خلد", "latinTranslit": "khālidīna"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فِيهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhyِf", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "fīhā"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "أَبَدًا", "translit": "āًdَbَaʾ", "root": "ابد", "latinTranslit": "abadan"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "رَّضِىَ", "translit": "َàِḍَّr", "root": "رضو", "latinTranslit": "raḍiya"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله", "latinTranslit": "l-lahu"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "عَنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿanhum"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَرَضُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḍَrَw", "root": "رضو", "latinTranslit": "waraḍū"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "عَنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnَع", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "ʿanhu"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "dhālika"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "لِمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmِl", "root": "", "latinTranslit": "liman"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "خَشِىَ", "translit": "َàِhsَhk", "root": "خشي", "latinTranslit": "khashiya"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "رَبَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّbَr", "root": "ربب", "latinTranslit": "rabbahu"}], "hasIhya": true, "haleemTranslation": "Their reward with their Lord is everlasting Gardens graced with flowing streams, where they will stay forever. God is well pleased with them and they with Him. All this is for those who stand in awe of their Lord", "clearyTranslation": "Their reward with their Lord is everlasting Gardens graced with flowing streams, where they will stay forever. God is well pleased with them and they with Him. All this is for those who stand in awe of their Lord"}], "tafsir": {"4": [{"arabic": "وهو أحد والآصال التي ذكرها الله تعالى إذ قال ولله يسجد من في السموات والأرض طوعا وكرها وظلالهم بالغدو والآصال وإذا سجد لله عز وجل الجمادات", "english": "I say: And just as knowledge and remembrance are the nourishment of the heart, which can never be cut off from it, so too the measure of moderation is this; and deficiency from it may lead to the disturbance of the body — except for one who accustoms himself to wakefulness gradually, for he may train himself upon it without disturbance. And this litany (wird) is among the longest of the litanies and the most delightful for the worshippers, and it is one of the \"evenings and mornings\" (al-āṣāl) which God Most High mentioned when He said: {«ولله يسجد من في السموات والأرض طوعا وكرها وظلالهم بالغدو والآصال»} \"And to God prostrates whoever is in the heavens and the earth, willingly or unwillingly, and their shadows, in the mornings and the evenings\" (Q. 13:15). And when inanimate objects prostrate to God, Mighty and Majestic, how then can the intelligent servant be heedless of the varieties of worship?", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 261", "section_title_en": "The Seventh Daily Litany (Al-Wird al-Sābiʿ): The Time of Sunset — The Completion of the Day's Litanies and Self-Accounting", "section_index": 261, "anchors": "- Root: ورد (warada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes arriving at water, seeking it, and drinking from it; also the portion or share of water allotted for drinking at a specific time.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الورود أصله: قصد الماء\" — the core meaning is the intentional approach to water; the litany (wird) is the appointed portion of devotional recitation, likened to the daily share of water that a person must take to sustain life.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): the act of arriving at water. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ورد علي كتاب سرني مورده\" — a book arrived upon me whose arrival pleased me; likewise, the wird is the arrival of the appointed time for worship, and the soul's approach to the fountain of divine remembrance.\n- Root: عصر (ʿaṣr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes pressing, squeezing, and extracting; also the time of afternoon, so called because it is the pressing of the day's end.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The ʿaṣr is the latter part of the day; God swore by it in {«والعصر»} (Q. 103:1), indicating its great merit.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the afternoon time. Metaphorical: the pressing of one's life — as the day is pressed at its end, so the life of man is pressed at its term.\n- Root: غرب (gharaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes disappearance, absence, and setting; the sun's setting (ghurūb) is its concealment from the eye.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The setting of the sun marks the boundary between the day's works and the night's; it is the terminus of the visible world's diurnal cycle.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the sun's descent below the horizon. Metaphorical: the setting of the sun of life — the death of man, after which there is no rising.\n- Root: حاسب (ḥāsaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes reckoning, counting, and taking account; muḥāsabah is the mutual reckoning between two parties.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-accounting (muḥāsabat al-nafs) is the soul's reckoning with itself over its deeds before the Day of Reckoning, for \"one who reckons himself before he is reckoned\" is among the wise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the arithmetic counting of sums. Metaphorical: the soul's audit of its own works, weighing the day's deeds against the standard of the Sacred Law.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the separation of the prepositional phrase from its governing verb, as in the construction \"فسبحان الله حين تمسون\" where the adverbial clause is interposed to emphasize the temporal specification of the act of glorification.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Insight"}], "5": [{"arabic": "الله عليه وسلم إنما الأعمال بالنيات وإنما لكل امرىء ما نوى // وقال تعالى وما أمروا إلا ليعبدوا الله مخلصين له الدين ولهذا قدم الله تعالى الصبر على العمل فقال تعالى إلا الذين صبروا وعملوا الصالحات الحالة الثانية حالة العمل كي لا يغفل عن الله في أثناء عمله ولا يتكاسل عن تحقيق آدابه وسننه ويدوم على شرط الأدب إلى آخر العمل الأخير فيلازم الصبر عن دواعي الفتور إلى الفراغ وهذا أيضا من شدائد الصبر ولعله المراد بقوله تعالى نعم أجر العاملين الذين صبروا أي صبروا إلى تمام العمل الحالة الثالثة بعد الفراغ من العمل إذ يحتا", "english": "The obedient one needs patience in his obedience in three states. The first state is before the act — namely, in rectifying the intention (taṣḥīḥ al-niyyah) and sincerity (al-ikhlāṣ), and in patience against the admixtures of ostentation (shawāʾib al-riyāʾ), the incitements of corruptions (dawāʿī al-āfāt), and in firmly resolving upon sincerity and fidelity. This is among the severest of patience for one who knows the reality of intention and sincerity, the corruptions of ostentation, and the wiles of the soul (makāyid al-nafs). The Prophet — peace and blessings be upon him — signaled this when he said, peace and blessings be upon him: \"Verily, deeds are but by intentions, and verily unto every man is only what he intended\" {إنما الأعمال بالنيات وإنما لكل امرىء ما نوى}. And God — exalted — said: \"And they were not commanded except to worship God, devoting religion purely to Him\" {وما أمروا إلا ليعبدوا الله مخلصين له الدين}. And for this reason God — exalted — gave precedence to patience over the deed, saying — exalted: \"Except those who are patient and perform righteous deeds\" {إلا الذين صبروا وعملوا الصالحات}.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 964", "section_title_en": "On Patience in Obedience and Abstinence from Disobedience — The Two Categories of Volitional Acts", "section_index": 964, "anchors": "- Root: صبر (ṣ-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of restraint and retention under constraint; al-Farahidi notes its application to holding back.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصبر: الإمساك في ضيق\" — restraint within hardship; the soul's confinement to what reason and Law require, or its abstention from what they require it to abstain from. It is a generic term whose specific names vary by context: in tribulation it is patience (ṣabr) against despair (jazaʿ); in battle it is courage (shajāʿah) against cowardice.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"صبرت على ما أكره\" (I bore patiently what I dislike) and \"صبرت عما أحب\" (I held back from what I love). Metaphorical (majāz): \"صبرت يمينه\" (I bound him by a stringent oath) — extended to any firm constraining.\n- Root: ع م ل (ʿ-m-l)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العمل: كل فعل يكون من الحيوان بقصد\" — every purposeful act of a living being; more specific than mere \"act\" (fiʿl), and applies to both righteous and wicked deeds.\n- Root: ن ف س (n-f-s)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The soul (nafs) as the seat of volition and desire; its innate inclination (ṭabʿ) is central to Ghazali's argument here.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"نفس\" as the self; metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه\" (he poured out his blood/life).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction and Focused Predication with innamā) — governing the Prophet's dictum «إنما الأعمال بالنيات» where the predicate is restricted exclusively to intention.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "8": [{"arabic": "وقال عز وجل {رضي الله عنهم ورضوا عنه ذلك لمن خشي ربه} وكل ما دل على فضيلة العلم دل على فضيلة الخوف لأن الخوف ثمرة العلم ولذلك جاء في خبر موسى عليه أفضل الصلاة والسلام وأما الخائفون", "english": "And He—mighty and majestic is He—said: {رضي الله عنهم ورضوا عنه ذلك لمن خشي ربه} \"God is well-pleased with them and they are well-pleased with Him; that is for whoever fears his Lord\" [Al-Bayyinah: 8]. And everything that indicates the virtue of knowledge indicates the virtue of fear, because fear is the fruit of knowledge. For this reason, it has come in the report concerning Moses—upon him be the most excellent prayer and peace—\"And as for the fearful...\"", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 1041", "section_title_en": "Exposition of the Virtue of Fear (Khawf) and Encouragement Thereof", "section_index": 1041, "anchors": "- Root: خوف (Khawf)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes trepidation, apprehension, and the state of being overtaken by dread of a feared outcome.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخوف: توقع مكروه عن أمارة مظنونة، أو معلومة\" — the anticipation of something detestable based on a probable or certain indication; it is the direct opposite of amn (security) and applies to both worldly and otherworldly matters.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"خفته على مالي خوفا وخيفة\" — fearing for something. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"تخوفه: تنقصه\" — to diminish gradually, as fear consumes the soul piecemeal.\n- Root: فضل (Fadl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Excess, superiority, or merit beyond the baseline of sufficiency.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفضل: الزيادة عن الاقتصاد\" — increase beyond moderation; the praiseworthy type is like the surplus of knowledge and forbearance, and it is measured by its end (ghāyah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"مال فلان فاضل: كثير يفضل عن القوت\" — surplus wealth exceeding necessity. The virtue of a thing is gauged by its instrumental efficacy toward a noble goal.\n- Root: علم (ʿIlm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Certain cognition and apprehension of a thing as it truly is.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — apprehension of a thing's reality; it is the root from which khawf branches as its fruit.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ما علمت بخبرك: ما شعرت به\" — to have cognizance. The scholars (ʿulamāʾ) are so named for their knowledge, which necessarily engenders fear of God.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (باب الحصر والتقييد) — governing the exclusive attribution in {إنما يخشى الله من عباده العلماء}, where the predicate restricts fear to the knowledgeable, and the syntactic fronting of \"whom they fear\" (لربهم يرهبون) emphasizes the object of reverential dread.", "content_type": "fiqh", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the inner and outer dimensions of worship, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Worship"}, {"arabic": "آلاف درهم لا أفعل ذلك أبدا رضي الله عنه", "english": "And a man came to Ibrahim ibn Adham with ten thousand dirhams, and he refused to accept them. The man pressed him, so Ibrahim said to him: \"Do you wish me to erase my name from the register of the poor for ten thousand dirhams? I shall never do that, ever.\" May God be pleased with him.", "book_title": "Ihya Ulum al-Din", "section_title_ar": "Section 1071", "section_title_en": "The Virtue of Poverty and the Condemnation of Wealth — Section 1071", "section_index": 1071, "anchors": "- Root: دخل (dakhala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of entering, penetrating, or intruding into a space or state.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Often used for entering into a condition or abode, including the afterlife states of Paradise and Hell.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical entry; metaphorical for entering into a spiritual state or reckoning.\n- Root: فقر (faqara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To hollow out, pierce, or be in a state of neediness; the poor one is \"hollowed\" of worldly goods.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Denotes the state of one lacking sufficient means; in the Kalam context, it signifies a spiritual station of utter dependence upon God, not merely material lack.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for material destitution; metaphorical for the soul's essential neediness before its Lord.\n- Root: غني (ghaniya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be rich, self-sufficient, or free from need.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological terms, absolute Ghina (Self-Sufficiency) belongs to God alone; human wealth is a relative accident (ʿaraḍ) that often breeds heedlessness.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for possessing abundance; metaphorical for the false sense of independence that veils one from God.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — Governing the syntactic flow of conditional and circumstantial clauses, ensuring the periodic structure of the narrative remains unbroken across multiple embedded quotations.", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Ihya Ulum al-Din'", "badge": "Commentary"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 200, 614], [2, 614, 1525], [3, 1525, 2891], [4, 2891, 4567], [5, 4567, 4981], [6, 4981, 5623], [7, 5623, 6689], [8, 6689, 9038], [9, 9038, 10725], [10, 10725, 11911], [11, 11911, 13463], [12, 13463, 15190]], "98:1": [[1, 200, 614], [2, 614, 1525], [3, 1525, 2891], [4, 2891, 4567], [5, 4567, 4981], [6, 4981, 5623], [7, 5623, 6689], [8, 6689, 9038], [9, 9038, 10725], [10, 10725, 11911], [11, 11911, 13463], [12, 13463, 15190]], "2": [[1, 200, 1603], [2, 1603, 2420], [3, 2420, 3441], [4, 3441, 5865], [5, 5865, 7409], [6, 7409, 9150]], "98:2": [[1, 200, 1603], [2, 1603, 2420], [3, 2420, 3441], [4, 3441, 5865], [5, 5865, 7409], [6, 7409, 9150]], "3": [[1, 200, 2186], [2, 2186, 3037], [3, 3037, 5080]], "98:3": [[1, 200, 2186], [2, 2186, 3037], [3, 3037, 5080]], "4": [[1, 630, 1946], [2, 1946, 3014], [3, 3014, 4437], [4, 4437, 6529], [5, 6529, 7640], [6, 7640, 8859], [7, 8859, 9290], [8, 9290, 9991], [9, 9991, 10789], [10, 10789, 13550], [11, 13550, 15350]], "98:4": [[1, 630, 1946], [2, 1946, 3014], [3, 3014, 44